[INAUDIBLE]

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.420
OK.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.790
Hi hey what's up, what
is Stauffer doing ?

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:16.560
Just basic tour
checking some stuff

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.315
we haven't done that today.

00:01:18.315 --> 00:01:22.560
Oh my ears just popped,
crazy air pressure.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.537
What is she doing
on this channel,

00:01:24.537 --> 00:01:25.620
she shouldn't be in there.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:40.024
[CLATTER] What the
fuck did you do.

00:01:40.024 --> 00:01:42.988
You can't-- sweetie are you OK ?

00:01:42.988 --> 00:01:44.530
Oh my god, you're
crazy [INAUDIBLE]..

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.900
See how savage you are.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.490
It's like completely different.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.870
I know I'm getting and
I didn't change the cold

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.070
but it's like here and there.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.560
Oh my god you got to try this.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.940
Eat this drink this,
breathe a little.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:01.850
Hi, everyone.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.840
My name is Rian Flynn and
I'm an undergraduate student

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.570
in Physics and
Theater Arts at MIT.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.070
Today it's my
pleasure to be joined

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:15.020
by Professor Jay Scheib for MIT
casts, three questions series.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.930
Hi, Hi Thanks Rian and
it's great to be here.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.890
My name is Jay Scheib
you just heard.

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:26.480
And I'm a professor of
theater arts, here at MIT.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.660
I just realized that I've been
a professor for theater arts

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:34.530
here now for 17 years, which
is kind of a shocker for me.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.900
It's great to be here.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:37.790
Thanks Jay.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:40.500
So to start off
the conversation.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.280
How do you use MIT
and its students,

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.760
to prototype and iterate
your off campus work,

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.230
and what are you able to do as
a director at MIT that you might

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:55.440
not be able to do elsewhere ?

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.110
OK, first question first--

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:05.190
You know I think like,
I think on average

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:08.280
in the old days on average--

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:14.130
I would do, I would do three
or four productions in a year,

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.700
in the summer especially I
would make things happen.

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:21.390
And, and doing them kind
of, kind of all over the US,

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.820
and primarily in Europe.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:29.400
And so at MIT my
courses would ultimately

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.220
be like an extension,
of my research process.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.930
And so I would develop
classes or seminars

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:40.750
that would kind of align
with whatever productions

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.770
I might happen to
have on the horizon.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:47.580
And whenever
possible, I would try

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:52.290
to develop aspects of
the work on campus,

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:56.100
and I've always found it to
be like a super rich process

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.850
to work with students
in that process.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.560
World of wires, the
excerpt that we just saw

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.100
was an example of
that sort of thing.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:09.970
Yeah in terms of
like what I can do

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:13.630
at MIT that can't
be done elsewhere,

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:17.980
that's like a super long list
because it's definitely not

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:22.570
every theater company in
the world where you can sort

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:28.240
of stroll down the hall and
bump into someone from Er

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.300
Astro for example.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.590
So being constantly confronted
with entirely different ways

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:40.870
of thinking and ways of seeing
and comprehending the world,

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:45.670
is something that really can't
be duplicated anywhere else.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:48.640
So that kind of level
of interdisciplinary

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.300
is shockingly important to me.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:55.430
Yeah I've definitely
seen how you've

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:59.010
collaborated with people in
Er Astro or other departments.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:02.450
And it's been incredible
to see, the work that's

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:05.240
come out of that.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:07.190
Jay and the two classes,
I've taken with you

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.523
at MIT I've greatly enjoyed
the times when you brought

00:05:09.523 --> 00:05:10.940
in your professional
collaborators

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.430
to teach like many
master classes,

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.050
and I've loved
being able to work

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:16.580
with your professional
collaborators

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:18.560
on projects like The Silence.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.160
What is it like to
combine MIT students

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:24.020
with your usual collaborators.

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.110
And could you speak to how
you bring these collaborators

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:27.950
into your teaching.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:35.765
Well-- So a lot of what,
a lot of what I've done--

00:05:35.765 --> 00:05:37.850
or obsessed with
over the years has

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:42.110
to do with working in, working
in theater or in opera which

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:47.150
are, which are two like really,
really old art forms, and also

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.360
sometimes incredibly
conservative art forms,

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:54.020
art forms with big walls
around them, especially opera.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:58.340
And, I've always been quite
obsessed with bringing

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:01.610
other disciplines to bear
on these potentially really

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:03.890
conservative forms.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:06.780
And that means that
often, I'm often

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:10.380
am working with people who have
wildly different skill sets.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:14.810
And so it's really kind
of an invaluable thing

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:18.230
to bring someone like, to bring
a cinematographer for instance

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:23.060
like Paulina Jurzec who you met.

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:25.370
But bringing her from outside.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:27.200
I would usually
only work with her

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:31.430
and in various
theatrical productions,

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:34.100
but bringing her
to the classroom

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:38.030
allows everyone to basically
like, get a perspective that's

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:43.160
really different from
mine, and learn new tricks.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.840
You know so I find, I find
that I find that it's just

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:48.700
a really expansive experience.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:53.430
And following on.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.210
The second part
of your question--

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.670
bringing-- you know
incorporating like into work

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:01.480
like The Silence.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:04.750
So we adopted Ingmar
Bergman's film

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:11.170
The Silence to a sort of live
cinema performance, which

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:14.320
we prototype in like to
[INAUDIBLE] like Fast

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.840
and furious weeks.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:22.150
And it was a mixture of
students and faculty and--

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:27.400
[INAUDIBLE] from around the
country, who participated.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.603
Now there was a time when I
would do this sort of thing,

00:07:30.603 --> 00:07:32.020
that where I would
think Oh gosh I

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:35.290
need to like kind of like
protect the students,

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:37.420
like not give them too
much, not like put them

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:40.450
at the center of the
thing, not like not--

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:42.250
put them in a
position where I don't

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:44.050
know they might have
a bad experience,

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:47.080
or feel somehow
like they weren't

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:49.450
able to keep up whatever.

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:53.920
And that's probably true
at some, some schools.

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:56.290
That might be a good
strategy, but I've

00:07:56.290 --> 00:07:59.190
found that at MIT
most of our students

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:04.130
like would totally, like not
even like allow that to happen.

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:09.400
So it's been exciting
to just throw people in.

00:08:09.400 --> 00:08:12.910
And you know, the results
of that kind of attitude

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:16.840
is that the students
rise to the occasion.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:20.650
Everyone tends to be incredibly
generous because there's

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:25.420
this like you know
grade like energy

00:08:25.420 --> 00:08:29.080
in the room from the
students, and the combination

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:31.690
is I think really
quite electric.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:33.970
And, and as a
result I've ended up

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:36.400
touring with students
that I worked

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:41.440
with, to places like the
Walker Art center, to Hungary,

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:46.510
to New York really
all over the place.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:52.360
So, that's really a great,
great symbiotic relationship.

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:56.620
And, and in the end,
everyone gets something from.

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:00.520
Yeah I definitely
enjoyed, acting as camera

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:02.260
operator on The Silence.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.270
When you just kind
of threw me in there

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:07.900
and had me with no
camera experience

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:10.900
just start operating a camera.

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:12.400
That was actually incredible.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:16.280
You had some experience you
had a little experience.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.850
And by the way, you
shot it beautifully.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:21.280
Thank you.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:24.790
Jay in your work you often
experience the intersection

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:27.490
of the real and the virtual.

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:30.820
And given that technology is
increasingly acting as such

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.180
a direct performance interface,
how does the cutting edge

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:37.480
research at MIT that you've
been involved in manifest

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.280
in your art ?

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.960
I think the biggest, the biggest
aspect or the biggest impact

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:47.170
that being at MIT has had
on my sort of engagement

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:50.960
with technology in what is
otherwise a super analog art

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:51.460
form.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:57.040
used to be kind of
fearless about it,

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:02.200
and to go ahead and ask
questions that at first glance

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:03.670
don't make sense. you know.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:06.130
Like does it make
sense for an audience

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:12.175
of 2000 people in an opera
house to all wear goggles.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:17.740
Probably not so we should try.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:20.470
And I think in a
way MIT has taught

00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:23.500
me to think like that for sure.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:25.690
But it's also given
me an opportunity

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:29.500
to like think in a way
quite incrementally.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:33.880
So the ways in which I've worked
with cameras over the years

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:37.720
has continually like
grown and expanded,

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:42.220
and, and I kind of like
focus in a studio practice,

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:46.300
on taking one step
after another.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:49.900
And I'm now in the process
of trying to figure out,

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:54.040
to what degree I can use
volumetric, volumetric

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:59.610
videography in order to create
an entirely different set

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:01.120
some sense of liveness.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:05.320
I'm no longer using cameras
but actually, but actually

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:07.510
capturing motion and light.

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:15.950
And finding ways to speed
that up to real time.

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:18.840
So in a way like,
I don't think I

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:21.220
ever I probably would
have never even heard

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.080
of volumetric filmmaking until
it had become mainstream,

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:29.770
had I not been in
the halls of MIT.

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:36.120
So it's-- it's kind of
a perfect relationship.

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:39.030
Can you speak to any upcoming
work that you're doing that

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.990
involves-- you
alluded to it in--

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:47.180
talking about 2000 people
in an opera house--

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:48.270
with goggles on.

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:51.360
Can you speak more to that.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:55.680
Yeah well I can and
I can't I guess.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.110
It hasn't been, hasn't
been announced because now

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:01.620
everything has been postponed.

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:06.510
But I have-- let's just have
two projects on the horizon,

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:09.060
both of which will--

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:12.540
both of which have
offered me an opportunity

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:16.950
to set a really high
challenge, which

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:22.860
is to find a way
to make use of AR,

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.380
VR technology and performance.

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:30.030
So augmented reality,
virtual reality

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:33.030
and some combination
of the two lights,

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:36.560
that we call extended reality.

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:42.090
So I've been sort of toying
around with them, starting

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:44.680
a group called an extended
performance cinemaography

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:45.180
group.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:50.520
Where we would sort of take
on the prospect of real time

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:53.250
effects processing and--

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:56.910
real time using laser scanners
and in completely different

00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:01.830
ways, in order to
develop performances.

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:06.060
And I and I'm going
to bring this research

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:10.380
to bear on, on a couple of major
operas in the next few years.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:13.020
[LAUGHING] I think
that's all I'm

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:15.090
allowed to say about
that particular project.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:18.540
But Yeah, so I'm working
on a Wagner opera.

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:25.420
I'm wondering, has
the coronavirus

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:28.780
and its implications
for social distancing

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:34.483
and the use of technology to
be having to use technology

00:13:34.483 --> 00:13:35.650
to interact with each other.

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:41.380
Has that influenced your
perception or thinking about

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:44.010
all these techniques that
you use in your work at all ?

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:53.202
Yeah it has, for
sure I think we may

00:13:53.202 --> 00:13:55.410
have an imperative in a way
like trying to figure out

00:13:55.410 --> 00:14:02.400
how to use the tools that we
have, to bridge the distances.

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.230
We do it pretty much
automatically all

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:08.580
the time right.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:10.380
But, but there's
something about,

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:12.210
there's something
about a live event

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:16.800
that you know I don't know,
does it for us in a way.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:19.770
Like when you're in a room
with someone who's performing.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:24.870
It's just a very
different experience,

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:26.460
than if you're say
watching a film,

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:32.740
or even watching a
video conference call.

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:35.650
But somehow, somehow
finding a way

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:39.230
to make technology bridge the
distances is an imperative.

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.530
And I can't think of a
better place than MIT

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:42.640
to try to figure that out.

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:48.130
You know we still don't
know when large assembly art

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:50.740
forms will--

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:54.850
come out of the, come
out of the basement.

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:57.460
Like right now we're
all kind of sheltered,

00:14:57.460 --> 00:15:00.340
so it's hard to say when
that will happen again.

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:04.070
Like when we're going to have
2000 people in an auditorium.

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:06.880
It may be that these people
that these 2000 people will need

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:11.350
to feel like they're in the
auditorium, and so you know I'm

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:14.890
very excited by developments
in like Olfactory

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:20.270
AR and other kinds of things
which would really give you--

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:24.380
a sense of being there,
even though you're not.

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:26.770
And hopefully without
the depression

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:30.230
when you realize that
you're actually not there.

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:31.860
Yeah.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:32.360
Yeah.

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:33.193
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:15:33.193 --> 00:15:36.115
Remember where are
you, you're in--

00:15:36.115 --> 00:15:36.740
where are you -

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:37.670
I'm remote

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:40.430
Being remote right,
there you go.

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:43.070
[INAUDIBLE]

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:47.450
Yeah, I remember you saying
once about how you know,

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:50.840
when you watch sports on TV.

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:53.720
It's a more live,
than live because when

